     5	Request (untrusted): {"request_id", "frame_b64", "sign_by"} or, for a large frame (IS-09), {"request_id",
     6	"frame_ref", "frame_sha256", "sign_by"}. Response: {"request_id", "status": "SIGNED" | "REFUSED", "sig_b64"?,
     7	"reason"?, "detail"?, "frame_sha256"?, "signer": {...}}.
     8	
     9	Policy, all before KMS is called (as the slot-2 signer, prototype/policy-signer/signer/main.py, and the issuer's
    10	StandInSigner):
    11	  - the request is exactly one of the two shapes; sign_by is required (IS-03);
    12	  - IS-09: a referenced frame is fetched from frames/<rid>/<sha256> and refused unless its SHA-256 is frame_sha256;
    13	  - the frame decodes, under the service verifier's own decoder, as tessera-v1/attest or tessera-v1/manifest;
    14	  - its alg is the hybrid pair (the kms slot exists only there);
    15	  - attest: signer.key_fp is this key's fingerprint; manifest: key_fp is this key's fingerprint;
    16	  - attest: |declared_issue_time - clock| <= EPS (24 h, A9.2);
    17	  - IS-03: the clock, read immediately before the KMS call, is not past sign_by nor the configured signing stop.
    18	After signing: the signature must pass the verifier's rule-R codec and verify over the full frame; and the clock is
    19	read again, so a signature KMS returned after the stop is withheld (SIGNING_STOPPED), logged, never sent.
    20	
    21	Transient failures (KMS or S3 errors, a failed self-check) raise Retry while the message has been received fewer
    22	than MAX_RECEIVES times, so SQS delivers it again; after that they are refused (SIGNING_ERROR). Every refusal is a
    23	response, never a silent drop. Every signature and every refusal is one structured log line. EPS and the policy are
