     1	# Where V1's signers and services run (2026-10-27)
     2	
     3	> **Status: OWNER DECISION**, by the owner instance (Claude Opus 5.5), 2026-10-27. It closes the "signer split"
     4	> item that the 2026-10-26 handoff left open as an author question. It changes no guarantee in `docs/V1-CONTRACT.md`. *(Corrected 2026-10-28: it first said it changed no
     5	> registered sentence. It changes K-R's enforcement wording in `formal/p8/HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT.md` (who computes `μ`,
     6	> who may `kms:Sign`); that is Amendment 11 §A11.5 item 5, for the author.)* Contestable by the author at any time.
     7	
     8	## Why this is the owner's call
     9	
    10	On 2026-10-24 the author agreed to put the AWS-slot caller in an Azure VM and the GCP-slot caller on Linode. On
    11	2026-10-26 the previous owner withdrew the reason it had given for that: a signer outside the issuing account still
    12	signs whatever the issuer sends it, so the split does not stop a captured issuer from getting signatures. Asked
    13	today what he wanted Azure and Linode for, the author said: *"I'd suggested them as places for the service. We have
    14	the linode instance, and I have a budget ($70/mo) via my MSDN subscription for Azure."* They were offered as
    15	resources, not as a requirement. Choosing among resources is owner work.
    16	
    17	## Decision
    18	
    19	1. **Slot 2 (GCP HSM, P-256) stays as it is**: the existing GCP policy signer, its own policy and its own logs.
    20	2. **The KMS slot (ML-DSA-65) is signed by a policy signer in the issuing AWS account**, under its own IAM role. That
    21	   role is the only principal granted `kms:Sign` on the key. The issuer roles keep no KMS permissions (already true,
    22	   `service/issuer/infra/main.tf:181`, checked by `tests/test_infra.py`). The signer reads frames from the existing
    23	   per-slot SQS queue and applies the same policy as slot 2.
    24	3. **CloudTrail**, with all management events, read and write, including KMS (`kms:Sign` is a management read event
    25	   in CloudTrail, not a data event; corrected the same day by the signer build), writes to an S3 bucket with Object
    26	   Lock in compliance mode. A captor can stop the trail from then on, but cannot delete what is already logged, and stopping the trail
    27	   is itself logged. This supports the AJ7 capture-notice recommendation. It adds no claim: V1 still promises no
    28	   detection.
    29	4. **No Azure or Linode in any signing path in V1.** Linode keeps serving wamason.com and never holds a signing role.
    30	   Azure is used for the **dead-service test** (4 Dec). A clean VM outside AWS verifies receipts with the Tessera
    31	   endpoint switched off. This uses the author's MSDN credit for the one V1 test that benefits from being off AWS.
    32	5. **A separate AWS signing account** (the previous owner's recommendation) is recorded as a **V2 candidate**, next to
    33	   the disjoint-authorities design (A9.3). Moving the KMS signer to another account later means redeploying
    34	   `service/signer-kms/infra` there with cross-account access to the queues and frames. That is a deployment change,
    35	   not a redesign. (Corrected the same day: this first said it was a change of one variable.)
    36	
    37	## Checks run on this decision
    38	
    39	- **Who can ask for a signature?** For the KMS slot: whatever can write to its SQS request queue (the issuer
    40	  roles), and any principal with IAM administration in the account, which can rewrite the key policy (`PutKeyPolicy`, held by the account root) to grant itself `kms:Sign`; an ordinary IAM grant cannot override the key policy's explicit Deny (corrected after the signer review, Q1). The full list of narrower routes, such as direct Lambda invocation and `iam:PassRole`, is in the signer READMEs and is checked at deployment (`service/issuer/DEPLOY.md` step 8). The same
    25	| G1 | **Exact bytes.** The receipt binds these exact bytes. Change one bit and the check fails. | C1 |
    26	| G2 | **Presented to Tessera.** The bytes were presented to the Tessera V1 service at some issuance. | C2 |
    27	| G3 | **Both signatures.** Both of the service's keys signed. A missing, stripped or swapped signature fails. | C3 |
    28	| G4 | **The keys were Tessera's.** DNSSEC (`_tessera-v1.wamason.com`, at the snapshot's own anchored time) and a signed git commit (at its own evidence time) named the keys for the service's domain. The git commit was in the Bitcoin chain at least 6 blocks before the receipt. | C4, A10.6 |
    29	| G5 | **Existed by a block.** The signed bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block: the lowest-height qualifying block in the presented proof, confirmed 6 deep. | C5, A10.1, A10.5 |
    30	| G6 | **A real-time bound, under an unchecked assumption.** A-UTC says at least one of that block and its 5 successors has a time stamp at most 2 hours behind when it was made. If A-UTC holds, the bytes existed before U (the latest of those 6 times, plus 2 hours). U is reported separately and never changes the verdict. | A10.1.1 |
    31	| G7 | **Window, two checks only.** The declared issue time is not before the keys' window opened, and the chain time T is not after it closed. This does not prove the signing happened inside the window. | C4, A10.2 |
    32	| G8 | **No Tessera needed.** The verdict depends only on the inputs above. | C6 |
    33	| G9 | **A typed verdict.** Once the trust configuration is authenticated, the verifier returns `VALID_STRICT`, `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE`, with a reason code and `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`, `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`, `standing: ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`. Missing required evidence prevents acceptance. A valid receipt still leaves everything in §3 unestablished. If the trust configuration is not authenticated, there is no receipt verdict. | C7; A9.5 trust-configuration authority; V1-PROFILE §5 (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`) |
    34	| G10 | **Honest receipts cannot be moved.** If an attacker takes over the DNS and git authority records while the service's own keys stay secret and unmisused, it cannot move honestly signed frames to other keys or bytes, or change their signer sets, whether they were signed before or after the takeover. | A10.4 |
    35	**No G11.** The service-key listing (A10.4.1) is not in V1, by the author's ruling of 2026-10-23 (E9 row 2,
    36	`docs/reviews/e9-packet/AUTHOR-RULINGS.md`; `docs/decisions/2026-10-19-listing-cut.md`). G10 holds while the independent release boundary of A9.5 holds.
    37	**New receipts under an attacker's own keys pass when both authority channels are captured.**
    38	
    39	## 3. Not guaranteed
    40	
    41	Each of these is a deliberate limit, not a missing feature.
    42	
    43	- **Who submitted the bytes**, or that a replayed receipt suits the current transaction (case f).
    44	- **What the bytes mean**, or that they are true. A contract inside the bytes is the checker's to verify (cases i, i′).
    45	- **That a key was not revoked** (cases e, e′).
    46	- **That an algorithm or a key was still safe** when it signed.
    47	- **Any lower time bound or priority.** "Existed by" is never "existed after" or "came first".
    48	- **Real time without A-UTC.** Without it, only chain order is claimed.
    49	- **Standing** of any kind.
    50	- **Separate control of the two keys.** One controller holds both. Capturing the issuing AWS account means both
    51	  slots can sign.
    52	- **Visibility.** Publishing the authority records is the issuer's duty; the verifier does not check it. V1
    53	  guarantees no detection or public exposure of a capture, of new receipts, or of a release given to one party.
    54	- **That DNSSEC shows publication before issue.**
    55	- **Anything after a Bitcoin reorganization of 6 or more blocks.**
    56	- **Renewal, degraded modes, or wrappers.** These are refused (`UNVERIFIABLE`), including honest ones (cases d2, h:
    57	  the two registered false rejects).
    58	- **One receipt per presentation.** A receipt id (SHA-256 of `A_sig`) names a receipt, not a presentation. Signing
    59	  is randomized, so the same honest frames can carry more than one valid signature set and therefore more than one
    60	  valid receipt, each with its own T. Each still meets G1–G10. (Owner, 2026-10-19, from finding E-02 of the first
    61	  real run.)
    62	- **Stability of T and U across different proofs.** A different proof or pin set can select a different block.
    63	- **Protection for a checker who skips authenticating the verifier release** or runs a substituted one.
    64	
    65	## 4. Failure model
    66	
    67	| Failure or attacker | What still holds |
    68	|---|---|
    69	| Tessera disappears | Everything in §2, if the holder kept the receipt and the verifier release. Reconstruction after the project dies is not demonstrated. |
    70	| Attacker alters a receipt or moves its signatures | Rejected (G1, G3, G10). |
    71	| Attacker uses its own keys, authority records not captured | Rejected: the required authority evidence does not name them (G4). |
    72	| Attacker captures both DNS and git authority records | Honest receipts protected (G10). New receipts under its own keys **pass** (the listing was cut). |
    73	| Attacker captures the issuing AWS account | **Not protected.** It can issue receipts that verify. |
    74	| Attacker steals the release key | Not protected: it can publish releases with a trust configuration of its choosing. This is distribution compromise, which A9 excludes (A9:47–52). A checker who installs no later release is unaffected. |
    75	| Bitcoin miners lie about time | G5 holds (chain order). G6 holds only under A-UTC. |
    76	| Bitcoin reorganizes fewer than 6 blocks | G5 holds. |
    77	
    78	## 5. Pairing: where each guarantee is checked
    79	
    80	The method needs every guarantee checked twice: once by a formal model or inspection, once by tests against the
    81	implementation. When the two disagree, we fix the contract, the model or the code. This is a first mapping, not
    82	yet checked case by case.
    83	
    84	**These are mappings, not pairs.** A pair, in the method's sense, runs the same scenario on both sides, so that a
    85	disagreement means something. Here the model side and the test side were written separately, for different
